  "id": "COMPOUND:1380 - PROTEIN:785",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that MMDA may interact with the Gastrin/cholecystokinin type B receptor through a series of intermediate targets. Specifically, MMDA is linked to the Mu-type opioid receptor, which is targeted by Nialamide, and Nialamide further targets the Gastrin/cholecystokinin type B receptor. Similarly, MMDA is also linked to the Alpha adrenergic receptor, which is targeted by Nomifensine, and Nomifensine also targets the same receptor. These findings indicate that MMDA may exert its effects via different pathways, potentially through distinct receptor interactions, ultimately leading to the activation of the Gastrin/cholecystokinin type B receptor. The integration of these pathways highlights the receptor's involvement in multiple signaling cascades, suggesting a complex regulatory role in physiological processes. The evidence supports the notion that MMDA may act as a modulator of the Gastrin/cholecystokinin type B receptor through various mechanisms, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
